<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vendel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vendel is a parish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Uppland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, where rich boat graves excavated from 1881 held helmets, weapons and animal sacrifices. The finds gave their name to the Vendel period of Swedish history, about 550 to 790, just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. Its decorated helmets resemble those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vendel.jpg" alt="Vendel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User IN433 on sv.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottarshogen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vendel lies in Tierp Municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Uppland</a> and overlooks Vendelsjön, a long stretch of water near the Vendel river, which joins the river Fyris. It was the site of an ancient royal estate belonging to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> öd, the network of estates that provided income for the medieval Swedish kings. Vendel Church was probably begun in the Romanesque style in the latter half of the 13th century and was given brick vaults around 1450. It is best known for its murals by Johannes Iwan, who worked in Uppland in the 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between 1881 and 1883 the Swedish archaeologist Hjalmar Stolpe uncovered 14 graves in and just beyond the south-east corner of the churchyard. Several were burials in boats up to 9 m long, identified by their many ship rivets, and they held fine swords and other weapons, helmets, cauldrons, chains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, shields and tools, with many animal sacrifices, mainly horses. One later grave, Grave 3, held an important set of bridle mounts. The graves date from the later 6th to the 8th century.</p><p>The helmets from Graves 1, 12 and 14 carry die-stamped plaques of warriors like those on the helmet from the early 7th-century ship burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>. The shield from Grave 12 has a stamped metal strip that is die-linked to an equivalent piece at Sutton Hoo, which points to a direct connection between the armourers who made the two sets of objects. The Sutton Hoo burial is often associated with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia">Rædwald of East Anglia</a>. A companion cemetery lies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valsgarde">Valsgärde</a> in the same region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>At nearby Husby stands a large mound that local tradition calls Ottarshögen, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohthere-of-halogaland">Ottar</a> or Ohthere, a figure also named in the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>. An excavation in 1917 found the remains of a powerful man buried at the beginning of the 6th century, the time of Ohthere; other graves of that date linked with his family lie at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamla-uppsala">Old Uppsala</a>. Vendel has also given its name to the Vendel style in art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vendel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scandinavia before 793 was a land of farms and chieftains, with rich boat graves at <b>Vendel</b> and Valsgärde in Sweden and trading places such as Ribe in Denmark, founded in the early 8th century, and Staraya Ladoga, about 753, on the route to the east.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Rich boat graves at <b>Vendel</b> and Valsgärde show a wealthy warrior elite.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-swedes">Kingdom of the Swedes</a></li></ul></section>
